module bindings.java.wildcardScopeCredentialRegistration;

// Apache HttpClient keeps credentials in a CredentialsProvider and matches
// them to requests by AuthScope: the credentials a provider hands out are
// served to whichever host the registered scope matches. AuthScope.ANY_HOST
// and AuthScope.ANY_PORT are that library's documented wildcard constants --
// a scope carrying the host wildcard matches every host the client contacts
// during the session, not only the endpoint the credentials were configured
// for. A build tool or client that resolves from several endpoints, or that
// follows a redirect, then presents those credentials to a host it never
// meant to authenticate to. That is CWE-522 with no tainted input anywhere in
// it: the leak is in how the credential store is scoped.
//
// The two facts this binding reads are both library vocabulary. The wildcard
// read: `AuthScope.ANY_HOST` / `AuthScope.ANY_PORT` passed into a call, read
// off the call-argument pair (`useCredentials:AuthScope.ANY_HOST` in the
// shape that motivates this), which keeps the needle on the argument a call
// actually receives rather than on any mention in the file. The registration:
// a `CredentialsProvider.setCredentials` call, read off the class's call
// paths. They live in different methods of the same class -- the wildcard is
// threaded through a helper's host and port parameters, so the registration
// site itself shows only parameter names -- which is why the anchor is the
// class, the one scope that sees both. Each class declaration in the file
// carries the flag, so a file with an inner class reports one flag per class
// declaration.
//
// The blanket-registration spelling `setCredentials(AuthScope.ANY, creds)`
// is deliberately not a needle. It is the library's documented blanket
// idiom, appears as ordinary code in single-endpoint clients, and says the
// wildcard at the registration site in plain text; this binding reads the
// host-axis wildcards threaded into a call, the shape where the registration
// site does not itself show the wildcard.
//
// Residual. A wildcard threaded through more than one helper hop before it
// reaches the scope constructor is still read, because the needle is the
// argument the call receives, not the scope's eventual use; a class whose
// context exceeds the frontend's class-scope token cap drops the pair
// entirely, the false-negative direction only. Prose cannot satisfy either
// clause: call-argument and call-path tokens are AST-derived, so a comment
// claiming host checks changes nothing.

binding javaWildcardScopeCredentialRegistration {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "class"
    and containsAny(node.context.callArg, ["AuthScope.ANY_HOST", "AuthScope.ANY_PORT"])
    and containsAny(node.context.callPath, ["setCredentials"])
  emit issue code.WildcardAuthScopeCredentialRegistration at node
}
